WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.121
Modern deep learning is full of feedback loops.

00:00:03.201 --> 00:00:08.094
A language model's next token depends on the tokens
it already generated.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:12.317
A robot's next observation depends on the action it
just took.

00:00:12.397 --> 00:00:17.834
But when we train these systems, we almost always
optimize a surrogate objective,

00:00:17.914 --> 00:00:22.806
like next-token prediction, that pretends the
feedback loop is not there.

00:00:22.886 --> 00:00:25.599
That surrogate tends to improve smoothly,

00:00:25.679 --> 00:00:29.277
which lulls us into thinking training is well behaved.

00:00:29.357 --> 00:00:32.479
The authors argue this is a dangerous illusion,

00:00:32.559 --> 00:00:37.792
and that the real culprit behind the instability is
not the data or the model,

00:00:37.872 --> 00:00:40.380
but the noise in the optimizer itself.
